# question: CQ-0007
# answers: Confusion between my mark and someone else's — which section is that?
# limits: Answers by routing, not by deciding. The Manual allocates the comparison elsewhere and this returns the sentence that says so with its passage; it does not assess any mark. Note the concepts named in `notLabel` — the retrieval trap here is that both destinations are about confusion, so a match on the word alone sends an examiner to the wrong test (GX-0005).

PREFIX tmk:  <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/>
PREFIX tmkg: <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/>
PREFIX skos: <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#>

SELECT ?record ?statement ?sourceRef ?allocatedTo ?modality
WHERE {
  GRAPH tmkg:approved {
    ?assertion a tmk:ApprovedAssertion ;
               tmk:goldRecord ?record ;
               tmk:assertionPredicate ?predicate ;
               tmk:assertionObject ?target ;
               tmk:supportingText ?statement ;
               tmk:sourcePassage ?passage .
    OPTIONAL { ?assertion tmk:modality ?modality }
    VALUES ?predicate { tmk:allocatesTo tmk:excludesBasis }
  }
  # Two GRAPH blocks over the same graph rather than one with two patterns, and
  # the split is a performance fix, not a semantic one: `tmkg:source` is a
  # constant IRI, so `GRAPH g { A . B }` and `GRAPH g { A } GRAPH g { B }` join
  # identically. What differs is how rdflib evaluates them — in one block it
  # builds the cross product of every `upstreamRef` in the source graph before
  # joining on the bound `?passage` and `?target`, which is quadratic in the size
  # of that graph. Written as two blocks each pattern joins against a variable
  # already bound, and the query goes from 409 seconds to 0.3 (Q-56).
  GRAPH tmkg:source { ?passage tmk:upstreamRef ?sourceRef . }
  GRAPH tmkg:source { ?target tmk:upstreamRef ?allocatedTo . }
}
ORDER BY ?record
